drvPExpert - i386 platform expert
=================================

One loadable driver bundle, PExpert.config, that carries every i386 bus
driver: the EISA / ISA Plug and Play bus (formerly drvEISABus), the PCI bus
(formerly drvPCIBus) and the PCMCIA bus (formerly drvPCMCIABus).  It is the
i386 counterpart of drivers-ppc/bus/drvPExpert in role, not in mechanism:
the ppc one is linked into the kernel, this one is loaded by the booter like
any other boot driver.  The kernel-linked half described in
docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-24-i386-platform-expert-design.md (chips/,
families/, discovery) can land beside it later as a second tool of the same
aggregate; the bus sources would then compile into that object unchanged.

Layout
------

    drvPExpert/
      apk/pkginfo                     drvpexpert, a kernel core package
      PExpert.drvproj/                the PExpert.config bundle
        Default.table                 one table for the whole bundle
        PExpert.lksproj/              PExpert_reloc
          PExpert.h, PExpert.m        the "Bus Class" and the settings lookup
          eisa/                       EISAKernBus, EISAResourceDriver, PnP
          pci/                        PCIKernBus, PCIResourceDriver
          pcmcia/                     PCMCIAKernBus, PCMCIAResourceDriver
        PnPDump.tproj/                the PnP dump tool EISABus.config shipped

The bus sources are copies of the three original projects with only the
changes the merge needed (below).  The originals stay in the tree until this
bundle has been boot-tested in their place; then they can go.

How it comes up
---------------

The booter loads PExpert_reloc for the "PExpert" entry in the System.config
"Boot Drivers" list and hands the kernel this bundle's table.  The kernel
sends probeBus: to the class named by "Bus Class", PExpert, which brings the
buses up in a fixed order - EISA first, because it is the kernel's default
bus and PCI delegates resource allocation to it, then PCI, then PCMCIA - and
claims the motherboard's own ports and the 8259 cascade IRQ on the EISA bus.
The kernel then probes each class in "Class Names", which registers the
EISA0, PCI0 and PCMCIA0 resource devices that Configure.app and driverLoader
use to auto-detect cards.

Two things moved out of the original drivers:

 * The motherboard resources.  Apple's EISABus table reserved
   "I/O Ports" = "0x00-0x0f 0x20-0x21 0x40-0x4b 0x70-0x71 0x81-0x8F 0x92-0x92
   0xc0-0xcf" and "IRQ Levels" = "2" through its EISA0 device.  With one
   table shared by three resource drivers the second driver's allocation
   would fail and the kernel would free a table the first still holds, so
   PExpert reserves them in code (reserveMotherboardResources) and the table
   carries no resource keys.
 * The per-bus settings.  Each driver used to look up its own table by
   "Server Name".  Both copies of that walk are replaced by
   PExpertServerAttribute(), which reads this bundle's table, so the keys now
   go in PExpert.config's Instance0.table:

       "PnP"                  = "No"  disables ISA Plug and Play
       "PnP Read Port"        = "0x203" fixes the PnP read port
       "Verbose"              = "Yes" PCMCIA bus logging
       "PCMCIA Memory Base"   = "0xD0000"
       "PCMCIA Memory Length" = "0x2000"

Adding a bus
------------

 1. Create a directory beside eisa/, pci/ and pcmcia/ holding a KernBus
    subclass whose +initialize registers it (registerBusClass:name:) and an
    IODirectDevice resource driver that registers "<Bus>0", in the pattern
    of pci/.
 2. Add the directory to BUS_DIRS in PExpert.lksproj/Makefile.preamble and
    the sources to CLASSES / CFILES / HFILES in PExpert.lksproj/Makefile.
 3. Add the resource driver to "Class Names" in Default.table.
 4. Add a bringUp:named:withTable: line to +probeBus: in PExpert.m, after
    the buses it depends on.

Drivers on the new bus name it with "Bus Type" in their tables; the kernel
turns that into the class name "<Bus Type>KernBus" and the device
description class "IO<Bus Type>DeviceDescription", so the KernBus subclass
must follow that naming and the description class must exist (the PCI and
PCMCIA ones live in driverkit-3).

Enabling it
-----------

Replace EISABus and PCIBus with PExpert in the "Boot Drivers" line of
/private/Drivers/i386/System.config (src/system_config-1/i386/Default.table):

    "Boot Drivers" = "PExpert PS2Keyboard Floppy ISASerialPort";

Never list PExpert together with EISABus, PCIBus or PCMCIABus: each bus
class registers itself under the same name and the second registration
replaces the first.

Status
------

Not yet built or boot-tested.  rbuild kernel --arch i386 builds this
project as a kernel core package (see rbuild-1/kernel.c), so an i386 profile
run is where it gets its first compile.  Not carried over from drvEISABus:
EISABusInspector.nib (a binary nib whose class is named for the old bundle)
and the reconstruction ledgers, which describe the original projects.
